result.rootCause:
src/lib/job-reschedule.ts:68 uses the ARRAY form `await db.$transaction([ ... ])`. The org-scoped tenant client rejects that form (src/lib/org-db.ts:65-100 throws "db.$transaction([...]) is not supported on the tenant client"), but the same proxy makes every model call EAGER (modelProxy, org-db.ts:38-51), so jobWorkSession.deleteMany, jobBreak.deleteMany and job.update inside the array literal have already been dispatched before $transaction is even called. Result: the three writes do land — unawaited, outside any transaction, minutes late — while the rejection skips the logActivity audit row (job-reschedule.ts:94-101) and is swallowed by the caller (src/app/admin/actions/saveJob.ts:1006-1008, `.catch(e => console.error("[saveJob] clearing the clock trail failed", e))`).

Smoking gun in the dev server log (line 20665 of the session dev.log):
[saveJob] clearing the clock trail failed Error: db.$transaction([...]) is not supported on the tenant client — the array form needs deferred Prisma promises and this client runs eagerly. Use db.$transaction(async (tx) => { await tx.x.y(); ... }) instead, and call everything inside it on `tx`, not on `db`.
    at async clearClockTrailForReschedule (...__2f59b2f1._.js:5003:5)
job-reschedule.ts:68 is the ONLY remaining array-form $transaction in src/ — budgetCategories.ts:284 and faqActions.ts:299 both carry comments saying they were converted to the interactive form for exactly this reason.

SECOND, INDEPENDENT GAP at src/lib/job-reschedule.ts:71-89: the job.update payload never touches JobAssignment, so status/clockInTime/clockOutTime/onMyWayAt on the per-cleaner row are never reset. With the sessions deleted, resolveClockEntry (src/lib/time-tracking.ts:95-130) falls back to the assignment's legacy clock pair and reports CLOCKED_IN with an open, ever-growing elapsed time.

FIX — replace src/lib/job-reschedule.ts:68-90 with the interactive form, calling everything on `tx`, and reset the assignment rows inside it:
await db.$transaction(async (tx) => {
  await tx.jobWorkSession.deleteMany({ where: { jobId } });
  await tx.jobBreak.deleteMany({ where: { jobId } });
  await tx.jobAssignment.updateMany({
    where: { jobId, status: { in: ["ON_THE_WAY", "CLOCKED_IN", "CLOCKED_OUT"] } },
    data: { status: "ASSIGNED", clockInTime: null, clockOutTime: null, onMyWayAt: null },
  });
  await tx.job.update({ where: { id: jobId }, data: { /* unchanged payload */ } });
});
(Alternatively call syncClockMirrors(jobId, { reconcile: cleanerIds }) from src/lib/work-sessions.server.ts:116 after the deletes — the same reconciliation updateClockTimes.ts:352 already performs.) Once the await is real, the logActivity row at line 94 will finally be written.
